@startuml proto-sr04
' ===========================================================================
' proto-sr04.puml - (a) the HC-SR04 ranging engine, both sides.
'
' STATUS: GREEN. tb_pe_soc_sr04.v passes on real RTL and the act is wired into
' regress/run_all.sh. An earlier revision of this figure drew the act as RED with
' five failing checks and a second measurement that never ran; that was measured
' and true when written, and the figure now records the supersession rather than
' quietly replacing it - see the note at the bottom.
'
' Measured, and every number in this figure is one of them:
'   image: 387 words loaded (637 NOP-filled)
'   run 0: echo 1160 us -> 1160 us -> 199 mm (expected 199 mm)
'   run 1: echo 5816 us -> 5816 us -> 999 mm (expected 999 mm)
'   run 2: echo 2000 us -> 2000 us -> 343 mm (expected 343 mm)
'   run 3: echo 8000 us -> 8000 us -> 1375 mm (expected 1375 mm)
' ===========================================================================

title HC-SR04 - the ranging engine on both sides\n<font size=12>STATUS: GREEN - 4 runs, 4 distances, 199 / 999 / 343 / 1375 mm, every one exact</font>

hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam state {
  BackgroundColor White
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
}
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
}
skinparam rectangle<<green>> {
  BackgroundColor #D6F5D6
  BorderColor #3A7A3A
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFBE6
  BorderColor #C8B800
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 11
}

state "STATUS: GREEN\n4 runs, 4 distances\n199 / 999 / 343 / 1375 mm" as OK <<green>>
OK : image: 387 words loaded (637 NOP-filled)\n\nrun 0: echo 1160 us -> 199 mm\nrun 1: echo  5816 us -> 999 mm\nrun 2: echo 2000 us -> 343 mm\nrun 3: echo 8000 us -> 1375 mm\n\n<b>ALL FOUR EXACT.</b> Run 3 is the interesting one:\n8000 = 64 x 125, so r = 0 and the\nconversion's truncation is a NO-OP.\n\n<b>TWO MUTATION CASES</b> of 61 in the family: sr-trig-count and sr-q-mask.\nThe first is caught ONLY by the 601 equality, because 600 clocks\nstill SATISFIES the device's "> 10 us minimum".

' ---------------------------------------------------------------- EMITTER --
state "FIRMWARE SIDE - firmware/sr04_range.pe" as EMIT {

  [*] --> PINS
  PINS : TRIG = pin 6 (SR_TRIG = 0x40), DRIVEN by us\nECHO = pin 5 (SR_ECHO = 0x20), RELEASED\nand READ BACK as the pad\n<b>TWO SEPARATE PADS, because the host drives one\nand listens on the other.</b> Writing a level and\nan enable together - the idiom the other acts\nuse - is impossible here, and the firmware does\nnot try: dmem[5] is the level and PINOE is the\nenable, and they are written on purpose.

  state "TRIGGER\n601 clocks" as TRG
  TRG : TXPIN = SR_TRIG, PINOE = SR_TRIG, then a\nCOUNTED delay, then release.\n\n<b>EXACT IN CLOCKS: 4 * SR_TRIG_LEN + 5</b>, and\nSR_TRIG_LEN = 149 makes that <b>601 clocks\n= 10.0167 us</b>.\n\n<b>SR_TRIG_LEN IS NOT SR_TRIG.</b> Both exist and\nthey are different KINDS of thing:\n  SR_TRIG      = 0x40  the PIN BIT - which pad\n  SR_TRIG_LEN  =  149  the DELAY COUNT - how long\n  SR_ECHO      = 0x20  the pin bit, read back\n  SR_RECOV_LEN =  117  the sensor's recovery wait\n\nA superseded revision of this figure\nprinted 4 * SR_TRIG + 4 = 600 clocks, which is\nwrong in THREE ways at once - the +4 should be\n+5, the count is 601 not 600, and the constant\nhas the wrong name - and wrong in the way this\nfamily has been repeatedly burned: plausible,\ninternally consistent, and sitting next to a\nformula it did not come from.\n\n<b>The testbench pins 601 as an EQUALITY</b>, so a\n600-clock derivation would have cleared every\ndatasheet window while failing the act's claim.

  state "RECOVERY\nSR_RECOV_LEN = 117" as REC
  REC : the sensor settles, and only then does\nECHO mean anything. Its ABSENCE was a real\ndefect once - a program that measures the line\nimmediately is reading the recovery - and the\nwait is now a COUNTED delay like every other\ntimed interval in the family.

  state "WAIT FOR THE ECHO'S RISING EDGE" as EUP
  EUP : spin on the pad until ECHO goes high.\n<b>THE ECHO IS CAPTURED, NOT SAMPLED,</b> which is\nwhat makes this act safe at 4 m: the answer is\nthe WIDTH of a pulse, and a width does not care\nwhen you looked at it.

  state "MEASURE\nt = the echo width in us" as MEAS
  MEAS : the 1 us counter read as a DIFFERENCE,\nexactly as in freqmeter.pe and for the same\nreason: the clear-on-read flag on port 6 is ONE\nBIT, so a poll loop longer than a tick reports\nseveral ticks as one.\n\n<b>THERE IS NO 16-BIT SUBTRACT HERE EITHER,</b> and for\nthe same reason as the meter: the counter is RESET\nat the echo's rising edge and READ at its falling\nedge, so the answer is whatever accumulated.\n<b>ONE RESET REMOVES BOTH THE SUBTRACT AND THE\nCOMPARE,</b> and this machine has no carry flag, so\na subtract is a four-case analysis on bit 7 and a\ncompare is the same analysis again.\n\n<b>Port 7 is the 4.33 us UART half-bit tick and is\nnot a microsecond.</b>

  state "CONVERT\nmm = us * 11/64" as CON
  CON : <b>THE CONVERSION IS THE ACT,</b> and it is where\nthe ISA bites. No multiply, no divide, no carry\nflag - only JZ/JNZ.\n\n  us = 64q + r\n  us*11/64 = 11q + 11r/64 = 11q + floor(11r/64)\n              ^^^^^^^^^   ^^^^^^^^^^^^\n              EXACT, because 11q is an integer\n\nSo no 16x16 multiply, no division and no\napproximation. The plan assumed a multiply;\nthis identity removes it.\n\n<b>EXACT MEANS TWO DIFFERENT THINGS HERE,</b> and\nconflating them is the defect - see the note\nat the foot of this figure.

  state "BANK\nthe answer" as BNK
  BNK : the answer for this run, and dmem[10]\ncounts the runs. <b>FOUR runs are banked and all\nfour match</b>; an earlier revision banked one\nand reported the second as an unwritten byte,\nwhich is what the act's last red state was.

  PINS --> TRG
  TRG --> REC
  REC --> EUP
  EUP --> MEAS
  MEAS --> CON
  CON --> BNK
  BNK --> PINS : the next run
}

' --------------------------------------------------------------- SENSOR/SRC --
state "SENSOR MODEL - tb/tb_pe_soc_sr04.v" as SRC {

  [*] --> ARM
  ARM : four targets, one per run, so the firmware\nis asked for four measurements and the model\nis re-armed between them.\n\n<b>WHY THESE FOUR, and it is not "more is better":</b>\n  1 160 us  a small target\n  5 816 us  exercises the ten-bit path AND the\n            high byte - an overflow here would be\n            invisible from the outside, because the\n            answer would still be a plausible\n            millimetre figure\n  2 000 us  a mid target\n  8 000 us  8000 = 64 x 125, so r = 0 and the\n            conversion's floor() is a NO-OP - the\n            one input where "exact" means exact with\n            nothing left over

  state "TRIGGER\ndetected" as TR2
  TR2 : each run's trigger is detected and\nanswered. The old act failed HERE: the model\nsaw 1 trigger where it had been given 2, and\nthe trigger was one clock short of the equality\nthe firmware's own derivation predicted.

  state "CHECKS" as CHK <<green>>
  CHK : per run, the firmware's measured width\nand its converted answer are compared with the\nmodel's, and the core is released fresh each\ntime so the second run cannot inherit the first\nrun's state.\n\n<b>AND the image length is COUNTED, not assumed:\n$readmemh into [0:1023] from a 387-word file\nleaves 637 words unwritten and says so only in a\nWARNING, so the testbench reads the file's own\nlength, asks $readmemh for exactly that many\nwords, and pre-fills the array with 16'hF000.\n\n<b>THE FILL IS CORRECT, NOT A DEFECT:</b> 0xF000\nis NOP and the firmware parks at JMP halt, so\nthe 637 words are a no-op region behind a program\nthat never returns there.

  ARM --> TR2
  TR2 --> CHK
  CHK --> ARM
}

EMIT -[#3A7,hidden]-> SRC : trigger out, echo in,\nand the answer is a NUMBER rather than\na waveform - the only act in the\nfamily whose output is a quantity

note bottom of CON
  <b>"EXACT" MEANS TWO THINGS IN THIS ACT, AND THE AMBIGUITY IS
  WORTH A FIGURE.</b>
  ...
  <b>1. THE IDENTITY IS EXACT.</b> us*11/64 = 11q + floor(11r/64) introduces no
  rounding error at all. That is a statement about the ALGORITHM, and it is the one
  the firmware's own header makes.
  ...
  <b>2. THE ARITHMETIC IS EXACT AND THE ANSWER IS TRUNCATED.</b>
  1160 * 11/64 = 199.375 is exact; <b>199 is floor of it</b>. So "PASS, an equality"
  is exact only AGAINST A TRUNCATING EXPECTATION - and the testbench's "expected 199"
  IS that truncation, so the two agree by construction rather than by luck.
  ...
  <b>3. RUN 3 HAS NO TRUNCATION.</b> 8000 = 64 * 125, so r = 0, floor is the\n  identity, and 1375 is the whole product - the one input where the word\n  means exact with nothing left over.\n  ...\n  <b>AND r = 0 DOES NOT MAKE IT A SHARPER TEST OF THE SMALL TERM. IT MAKES\n  IT BLIND TO IT</b> - the opposite of what an earlier revision claimed.\n  With the small term forced to 0: 1160 gives 198 (WRONG), 5816 gives 990\n  (WRONG), 2000 gives 341 (WRONG) and <b>8000 still gives 1375</b>. The small\n  term contributes 1, 9, 2 and <b>0</b> respectively.\n  ...\n  <b>SO RUN 3 DOES NOT TEST THE SMALL TERM AT ALL - IT ISOLATES THE BIG ONE,</b>\n  and that is what earns it its place.\n  ...\n  <b>IT IS SHARP IN THE OTHER DIRECTION, which is where sr-q-mask comes\n  from:</b> the conversion masks the high byte of us with 0x3F, and those high\n  bytes are 0x04, 0x16, 0x07 and 0x1F - <b>bit 3 is set in 0x1F ALONE</b> - so a\n  slip in that mask is invisible in three runs and caught only here, where Q\n  falls 125 -> 93 and the answer 1375 -> 1023.\n  ...\n  <b>AND IT IS THE ONLY WIDTH WHOSE us x 11 OVERFLOWS 16 BITS:</b>\n  8000 x 11 = 88 000 against a 65 535 ceiling. The nearest miss is\n  5816 x 11 = 63 976, 1 559 short of it - so 5816 and 8000 BRACKET the\n  boundary rather than sitting on one side of it.
  ...
  A figure or a page that said only "the conversion is exact" was making claim 1 and
  being read as claim 2. That is the defect, and the four-run table above is what
  makes it impossible to reproduce.
end note

note bottom of BNK
  <b>TWO MUTATION CASES, AND THE DIRECTION EACH EXERCISES MATTERS.</b>
  ...
  regress/mutate_timing_tb.sh carries 62 cases across the timing family and TWO
  are sr04. An earlier revision of this figure said none; that was true when
  written and is corrected here rather than dropped.
  ...
  | case | the change | what catches it |
  | <b>sr-trig-count</b> | deletes the redundant LDI that sets the trigger's ending level - the loop's last SUB has already left A at zero | <b>ONLY the 601 equality.</b> The mutant gives 600 clocks = 10.000 us, which SATISFIES the device's "> 10 us minimum", and the firmware stays otherwise perfect: same four answers, same widths, same one trigger per run. <b>A WINDOW CHECK PASSES IT.</b> |
  | <b>sr-q-mask</b> | drops ONE bit from the 0x3F mask on the high byte of us | 1375 -> 1023 <b>on 8 000 us ALONE</b> - the high bytes are 0x04, 0x16, 0x07 and 0x1F, and bit 3 is set in 0x1F alone |
  ...
  <b>sr-trig-count IS THIS ACT'S REAL CLAIM IN ONE LINE:</b> the trigger is exactly
  601 clocks and nothing in the repository holds it to that except an equality.
  Every other act in this family is judged by a WINDOW, and a window passes the
  mutant. That is also why the measured count is written down as 601 rather
  than as "10 us".
  ...
<b>AND THE SMALL TERM'S CASE IS WRITTEN BUT NOT MERGED.</b>\n  Nothing MERGED perturbs the small term, floor(11r/64) - the mutant that\n  would do it is invisible in exactly the run one might expect (CON note\n  above). But <b>sr-term-shift</b> (e6ff51d) exists and is the COMPLEMENT of\n  sr-q-mask: sr-q-mask reaches only the r = 0 run, sr-term-shift only the\n  r != 0 ones, so between them they cover every width in the set.\n  <b>It is not an ancestor of main and not in the tree this figure was\n  written against</b> - so that coverage is PENDING, not HELD. Writing it as\n  held would be the rounding error this figure exists to avoid.
end note


note bottom of TRG
  <b>WHAT THIS FIGURE USED TO SAY, and why it is recorded rather than
  deleted.</b>
  ...
  An earlier revision drew the act as RED - five failing checks, a second measurement
  that never ran, an answer of X for an unwritten byte - and printed the trigger as
  4 * SR_TRIG + 4 = 600 clocks = 10.000 us. That was MEASURED, not assumed, and it was
  wrong in a specific instructive way.
  ...
  It was wrong about the count, about the +4 versus +5, and about which of two
  same-valued constants was meant - and it was wrong in the shape this branch has been
  burned by repeatedly: internally consistent, plausible, and derived from something
  other than the formula printed a few lines away. The corrected form is above and
  the testbench pins 601 as an equality, so the wrong derivation would have cleared
  every datasheet window while failing the act's own claim.
end note

@enduml
